            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to someone's face, then the Mandarin for a hard lesson learned."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 错过",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "错过 — cuòguò — \"to miss a chance; to miss a train\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我错过了那个机会 wǒ cuòguò le nàge jīhuì — I missed that chance."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 错 (cuò) — thirteen strokes in two pieces: on the left the metal piece of 钱, five strokes; on the right a short bar with two verticals through it and a long bar under them, then 日 rì, eight strokes. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "错过 (cuòguò) is built from 错 (cuò) 'wrong' and 过 'to pass', both inherited from Middle Chinese, tshak and kwaH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'a file' and 'mixed', and the second 'a mistake' and 'to pass over'. How the first came to mean wrong is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for giving reasons."
            }
          ]
        },
